Here's the insert function using pgx:

```go
func InsertComment(ctx context.Context, db *pgxpool.Pool, postID int, body string) (Comment, error) {
	body = strings.TrimSpace(body)
	if body == "" {
		return Comment{}, fmt.Errorf("insert comment: body must not be empty")
	}

	const q = `
		INSERT INTO comments (post_id, body)
		VALUES ($1, $2)
		RETURNING id, post_id, body, created_at`

	var c Comment
	err := db.QueryRow(ctx, q, postID, body).Scan(
		&c.ID, &c.PostID, &c.Body, &c.CreatedAt,
	)
	if err != nil {
		var pgErr *pgconn.PgError
		if errors.As(err, &pgErr) && pgErr.Code == "23503" {
			// foreign_key_violation: post_id doesn't exist
			return Comment{}, fmt.Errorf("insert comment: post %d not found", postID)
		}
		return Comment{}, fmt.Errorf("insert comment: %w", err)
	}
	return c, nil
}
```

Notes: RETURNING lets the database hand back the generated id and created_at default in the same round trip, so there's no second query and no clock-skew between app and DB timestamps. I special-case the 23503 foreign-key violation into a clear 'post not found' error since that's the one bad-input case a caller can act on; everything else wraps with %w so the original pgx error is still inspectable up the stack. The context parameter means the query is cancelable if the request is aborted.

